<SiteLayout title="8th Pay Commission Consultations: Cities and Next Steps" description="8th Pay Commission consultation meetings in Jaipur, Chandigarh, Bengaluru, Mumbai and Delhi: status, how to request a meeting and what happens after the report." slug="8th-pay-commission-consultations" h1="8th Pay Commission Consultations 2026" lead="Where the commission has met stakeholders, and the path from report to payment." faq={faq} toc={toc} related={related} pageType="article" breadcrumbLabel="Consultations">

<div class="answer"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> The 8th Pay Commission is holding consultation meetings with employee associations, pensioner bodies and departments in several cities. Reported visits include Jaipur, Chandigarh, Bengaluru, Mumbai and Delhi. As of 15 November 2026 the consultations are continuing, and no recommendation has been announced.</p></div>

<h2 id="status">Where things stand</h2>
<ul>
<li>The commission was constituted on 3 November 2025 and has 18 months to report, which points to about May 2027.</li>
<li>The online memorandum window closed on 15 June 2026. See <a href="/8th-pay-commission-memorandum-submission/">memorandum submission</a>.</li>
<li>Meetings were held earlier in Jaipur and Chandigarh, and the commission's website shows meetings in Mumbai and Bengaluru more recently.</li>
<li>Associations that want a direct meeting must follow the appointment procedure on the commission's official website, where meeting notices give dates and venues.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="why">Why consultations matter</h2>
<p>These meetings let unions, pensioner groups and departments explain pay, allowance and pension problems in person after sending written memoranda. They feed into the report but do not decide it.</p>

<h2 id="after">What happens after the report</h2>
<p>A report does not mean higher pay the next month. The usual path is:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What happens</th><th>Who acts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">1. Report</th><td>The commission submits its report to the Union Government (due about May 2027).</td><td>Commission</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2. Examination</th><td>The government studies the financial cost and administrative effect of each recommendation.</td><td>Finance Ministry, DoPT</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">3. Decision</th><td>The recommendations are accepted, modified or considered separately. The Cabinet decides.</td><td>Union Cabinet</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">4. Orders</th><td>Ministries issue implementation orders and revised pay rules.</td><td>Ministries and departments</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">5. Payroll</th><td>Pay and pension systems are updated and arrears are worked out.</td><td>Pay and accounts offices</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>This gap is why implementation is not expected in the month the report arrives. See the likely timeline on <a href="/when-will-8th-pay-commission-start/">when it will start</a> and the <a href="/8th-pay-commission-implementation-date/">implementation date</a>.</p>

<h2 id="watch">What to watch</h2>
<ul>
<li>Notices on 8cpc.gov.in for new meetings.</li>
<li>Any interim report on a narrow topic.</li>
<li>The January 2027 DA order. See <a href="/da-hike-january-2027/">DA hike January 2027</a>.</li>
</ul>

